The problem the program must solve
Decide, from three sensor readings, whether there is a real fire, and warn people at once while 
avoiding false alarms.
INPUTS
• Smoke: MQ-2, 0 to 1023
• Flame: IR sensor, 0 to 1023
• Temperature: DHT11, in °C
PROCESS
• Clean the readings
• Compare each with its limit
• Count the sensors that agree
• Hold the level steady
OUTPUTS
• Alert level: 4 levels
• LEDs and buzzer
• Smoke-extracting fan
• LCD text and phone message
2

System design: who does what
Input
Smoke, flame and heat 
in the room
Sensor / interface
MQ-2, IR flame sensor, 
DHT11
Microcontroller
Arduino Uno/Nano 
running the firmware 
(C++)
Processing
Python program on the 
laptop
Output / actuator
LEDs, buzzer, fan via 
relay, LCD, Bluetooth
USB serial: one reading per second in, one command out
Arduino (C++)
• Reads the three sensors every second
• Switches LEDs, buzzer, fan relay and LCD
• Sends the alert level to the phone
• Fail-safe: simple local rule if Python goes silent
Python (laptop)
• Reads and checks each data line
• Smooths the readings and compares them with limits
• Counts the sensors that agree and holds the level
• Chooses the outputs and logs every cycle
Why split the work: Python is easy to read, test and change without re-uploading code, and the Arduino keeps direct control of the hardware.
